{# Replaces the prompt of tacticmedia/qa-bundle 2.0.0, which has no blocks to extend. -#}
A reviewer examined the screenshots of the latest journey run and wrote the
visual feedback below: incorrect layout, incorrect colours, insufficient contrast,
and rendering errors. Correct each reported item.

Read AGENTS.md in the root of this repository before you make a change, and follow
it. Its conventions have precedence over other conventions. Its hard rule applies to
each item: an item that asks for more than a local fix, for example a new behaviour,
a new screen state, or a contract change, gets a row in docs/plan/features.md and a
scope file docs/plan/Fnn-<name>.md before any code. Correct a local item directly.

To find the code for a screenshot: each image comes from a journey spec in
tests/journeys/, a Playwright Test spec that runs the SPA against the mocked API of
tests/ui/support/. "FILE" is the spec, "SCENARIO" is its test, and "SCREEN" is the
label of a captureScreen() call. Search the spec for that label to find the point in
the journey at which the screen was reached, then read the code that renders it.
This command runs the journey of one spec again:

    npx playwright test --config playwright.journeys.config.ts <FILE>

A run empties var/screenshots first, so it removes the screenshots of the other
specs. `make journeys` writes all of them again.

To read a note: "Area" is the region that the reviewer selected, in natural image
pixels of a full-page capture. x starts at the left edge of the page, and y starts
at the top of the page, not at the top of the viewport. "Whole screen" means that
the note applies to the complete screen. Read the cropped view first, where a red
outline marks the selection, and open the full screenshot for the surrounding
context.

To find a selection in the code: "Page" is the URL that the journey was on when it
made the capture. Find its path in assets/router.tsx to find the route component.
Then search for the element text, for the class strings, and for the structure that
the selector describes.

That URL is the `vite preview` server that the journey run started on JOURNEY_PORT.
It stops at the end of the run. The fakes of the spec supply its data, so the same
route on the dev stack shows different records or answers 404.

{% for class, screens in entries %}
================================================================================
JOURNEY:  {{ class }}
{% for name, rows in screens %}
{% set screen = rows|first.screen %}
{% set origin = rows|first.metadata %}
{% if screen %}
{% if origin and origin.testFile %}
FILE:     {{ origin.testFile }}
{% endif %}
SCENARIO: {{ screen.method }}
SCREEN:   "{{ screen.label }}" (capture #{{ screen.sequence }} in the scenario)
{% else %}
SCREEN:   {{ name }} (filename could not be parsed)
{% endif %}
{% for row in rows %}

  - Screenshot: {{ row.path ?? (row.item.name ~ '.png (no longer in the tree)') }}
    Mode: {{ row.item.mode }} | Viewport: {{ row.item.viewport }} ({{ row.orientation }})
{% if row.item.wholeScreen %}
    Area: whole screen
{% else %}
    Area: x={{ row.item.rectangle.x }}, y={{ row.item.rectangle.y }}, width={{ row.item.rectangle.width }}, height={{ row.item.rectangle.height }} (image is {{ row.item.imageSize.width }}x{{ row.item.imageSize.height }} px)
{% endif %}
{% if row.metadata %}
    Page: {{ row.metadata.url }} ("{{ row.metadata.title }}")
{% endif %}
{% if row.crop %}
    Cropped view (selection outlined in red): {{ row.crop }}
{% endif %}
{% if row.resolution and row.resolution.layoutChanged %}
    Warning: the page size changed after this note was written, so the coordinates
             and the crop do not show the reported area. Use the note text and the
             page above.
{% elseif row.resolution %}
{% if row.resolution.selected is not empty %}
    The selection contains:
{% for element in row.resolution.selected %}
      - <{{ element.tag }}>{{ element.text ? ' "' ~ element.text ~ '"' : '' }}
        selector: {{ element.selector }}
{% if element.classes %}
        classes: {{ element.classes }}
{% endif %}
{% for attribute, value in element.attributes %}
        {{ attribute }}: {{ value }}
{% endfor %}
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}
{% if row.resolution.enclosing is not empty %}
    Inside:
{% for element in row.resolution.enclosing %}
      - <{{ element.tag }}> {{ element.selector }}
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}
{% endif %}
{% if row.stale %}
    Note: this file is not in the current run, so it comes from an earlier run. The
          journey, scenario and screen above still identify the reviewed screen.
{% endif %}
    Feedback: {{ row.item.note }}
{% endfor %}
{% endfor %}

{% endfor %}
================================================================================

Instructions:

1. Examine the named screenshot for each item before you change the code.
2. Correct the item in the component or the CSS module under assets/ that renders
   the screen. Do not change the journey to hide the defect.
3. Keep light and dark mode equivalent. Check a correction in both modes, and at
   each captured viewport, because the same screen exists at all of them.
4. Use the conventions of this repository for markup, styling, wording and
   client-side behaviour: Primer components and CSS modules. Do not introduce new
   conventions.
5. If you change user-facing behaviour, update the journey of that screen and the
   UI test of that behaviour in tests/ui/ in the same change.
6. When the corrections are complete, run `make journeys`, examine the new
   screenshots at /_dev/screenshots, and run `make check`.
